# 12. Factory vs Model

This distinction is very important.

Your model:

```text
app/Models/Project.php
```

represents your application's database entity.

Your factory:

```text
database/factories/ProjectFactory.php
```

describes how to generate fake instances of that entity.

Think:

```text
Project Model
      │
      │ represents
      ↓
projects table


Project Factory
      │
      │ generates fake data for
      ↓
Project Model
```

---

# 13. How Laravel Connects Factory to Model

The factory contains:

```php
protected $model = Project::class;
```

This means:

```text
ProjectFactory
      ↓
Project::class
      ↓
App\Models\Project
      ↓
projects table
```

Modern Laravel's generated factories also follow conventions that allow:

```php
Project::factory()
```

to locate the corresponding factory.

---

# 14. Important Requirement: `HasFactory`

Your model normally uses the `HasFactory` trait:

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Factories\HasFactory;
```

Then:

```php
class Project extends Model
{
    use HasFactory;

    protected $fillable = [
        'project_name',
    ];
}
```

Complete model:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Factories\HasFactory;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Project extends Model
{
    use HasFactory;

    protected $fillable = [
        'project_name',
    ];
}
```

The important part is:

```php
use HasFactory;
```

and:

```php
use HasFactory;
```

inside the class.

This gives the model access to:

```php
Project::factory()
```

---

# 15. Complete Connection

Now everything connects:

```text
Project.php
    │
    │ use HasFactory
    ↓
Project::factory()
    │
    ↓
ProjectFactory.php
    │
    │ definition()
    ↓
fake project data
    │
    ↓
Project model
    │
    ↓
projects table
```

