# 16. Create One Record

You can use:

```php
Project::factory()->create();
```

This:

```text
1. Generates fake data
2. Creates a Project model
3. Inserts the model into the database
4. Returns the Project model
```

Example:

```php
$project = Project::factory()->create();
```

You can then access:

```php
$project->id;
$project->project_name;
```

---

# 17. Create Multiple Records

Create 10 projects:

```php
Project::factory()->count(10)->create();
```

Or:

```php
Project::factory(10)->create();
```

Both represent:

```text
Create 10 Project records
```

---

# 18. `make()` vs `create()`

This is very important.

## `make()`

```php
$project = Project::factory()->make();
```

Creates the model in memory.

It does NOT insert the record into the database.

Conceptually:

```text
Factory
   ↓
Project object
   ↓
Memory

NO DATABASE INSERT
```

---

## `create()`

```php
$project = Project::factory()->create();
```

Creates the model and saves it.

```text
Factory
   ↓
Project object
   ↓
Database INSERT
```

So:

```text
make()
    = create model in memory

create()
    = create model + save to database
```

---

# 19. Creating Multiple Models with `make()`

```php
$projects = Project::factory()
    ->count(10)
    ->make();
```

This gives you a collection of models in memory.

No records are inserted.

---

# 20. Creating Multiple Models with `create()`

```php
$projects = Project::factory()
    ->count(10)
    ->create();
```

This inserts 10 rows.

---

# 21. Override Factory Values

Suppose your factory is:

```php
public function definition(): array
{
    return [
        'project_name' => fake()->sentence(3),
    ];
}
```

Normally:

```php
Project::factory()->create();
```

generates a random project name.

But you can override it:

```php
Project::factory()->create([
    'project_name' => 'Employee Management System',
]);
```

The factory still generates all other fields, but:

```text
project_name
```

will use your supplied value.

---

# 22. Create Several Specific Projects

You can do:

```php
Project::factory()->create([
    'project_name' => 'Employee Management System',
]);

Project::factory()->create([
    'project_name' => 'Inventory Management System',
]);

Project::factory()->create([
    'project_name' => 'Payment Management System',
]);
```

---

# 23. Factory States

Factories can also have reusable states.

Suppose you want:

```text
small project
large project
internal project
external project
```

You can define methods on the factory.

For example:

```php
public function internal(): static
{
    return $this->state(fn (array $attributes) => [
        'project_type' => 'internal',
    ]);
}
```

Then:

```php
Project::factory()
    ->internal()
    ->create();
```

This becomes useful when your table has additional columns representing different states.

---

# 24. Example with More Columns

Suppose your projects table eventually becomes:

```text
projects

id
project_name
description
start_date
is_active
created_at
updated_at
```

Your factory could be:

```php
public function definition(): array
{
    return [
        'project_name' => fake()->sentence(3),
        'description' => fake()->paragraph(),
        'start_date' => fake()->date(),
        'is_active' => fake()->boolean(),
    ];
}
```

The factory should normally provide values for the columns that you want automatically generated.

You don't need to generate:

```text
id
created_at
updated_at
```

because Laravel/database handles those automatically in the normal setup.

---

# 25. Factory with Relationships

Factories become especially powerful when you have relationships.

Your current project has:

```text
Department
    |
    | hasMany
    ↓
Employee
    |
    | belongsToMany
    ↓
Project
```

Suppose Employee has:

```php
department_id
```

A factory can generate related records.

For example:

```php
Employee::factory()
    ->for(Department::factory())
    ->create();
```

Conceptually:

```text
Create Department
       ↓
Get Department ID
       ↓
Create Employee
       ↓
Set employee.department_id
```

This is one of the major reasons factories are useful.

